sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, ADR 0241 allowlists, DECISIONS R1-R4 + 5 (process.stop shape, shared SSH identity).
  %% status: reviewed
  %% command: firewall:allow
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: firewall:allow 5432<br/>--node=7 --from=CIDR --label=…
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit firewall:allow 5432 --node=7 --from=10.6.0.0/24 --label="office postgres"`
  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp] [--label=TEXT]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the port as the argument: a number such as `5432`, or a range such as `8000:8100`
  %%> - the Node, and optionally the source address and the protocol
  %%> - optionally a label or short description
  %%> Not accepted:
  %%> - service names such as `postgres` as the port
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, record the rule<br/>rule: provisioning · task #5130
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 7
  %%> - Node 7 is an active Linux Node
  %%> - the port and source are valid
  %%> - Node 7 has no rule yet for the same port, protocol and source
  %%> - no opposite rule for the same port, protocol and source; an allow and a deny for exactly the same scope is refused (`firewall.rule_conflict`)
  %%> Allowed:
  %%> - overlapping rules with a different scope, for example deny `5432` from anywhere plus allow `5432` from one IP
  %%> - no other firewall change runs on Node 7; the Gateway holds one lock per Node
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the rule for port 5432 recorded with status provisioning
  %%> - task #5130 recorded with the full intent
  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-firewall-direct
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the rule as exact ufw lines: port, protocol, source, direction
  %%> - Node 7's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5130
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Add the ufw rule<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall apply
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 7 over SSH from 209, with the official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall` behavior
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adds one ufw allow line per port and protocol, commented with its label
  %%> Order:
  %%> - narrower rules go above broader ones, allow or deny, so an exception such as one IP wins over a rule for anywhere
  %%> - ufw stops at the first match, so Orbit sets this order on every change
  %%> - all operator rules sit above Orbit's own broad rules, such as WireGuard peer trust, so a deny on a port also applies to WireGuard peers
  %%> - an existing identical line is left as it is
  SW->>N: Check the rule is live<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads ufw back and checks every expected line is present
  %%> - checks that narrower rules sit above broader ones for the same port
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the rule only counts as active when ufw shows it
  alt every line is present
    N-->>SW: ufw lines present
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the check passed for every line
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result<br/>rule: active · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the rule for port 5432 becomes active
    %%> - task #5130 succeeds
  else adding or checking fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0
    %%> When:
    %%> - ufw refuses the line, SSH fails, or a line is missing after the add
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Mark the rule failed<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the rule for port 5432 is marked failed, so you can see it
    %%> - task #5130 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - lines added before the failure stay; there is no rollback
    %%> - that is safe: the rule is marked failed, and a rerun skips lines that already exist
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5130
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
